    {
      "id": "risk_rating_bands",
      "type": "values",
      "description": "The risk rating bands of the risk score (asset side x vulnerability side): a finding scoring at least the first value is Critical, at least the second High, at least the third Moderate, below that Low. Three whole numbers, highest first. Owner 2026-10-11: 50 / 30 / 15 (from the real score distribution: a tier-0 asset with a critical vulnerability, 56, is Critical); the values are data so they change without a release. Customer: lists.custom.json -- Pro: the list's override.",
      "values": [
        50,
        30,
        15
      ],
      "labels": [
        "Critical",
        "High",
        "Moderate"
      ],
      "below": "Low"
    },
    {
      "id": "break_glass_patterns",
      "type": "values",
      "description": "The NAMING PATTERNS of an emergency-access (break-glass) account -- break glass is a CLASSIFICATION, not a risk factor. Glob patterns (* = any text, ? = one character; letters, digits, space and . _ @ -; never wildcards only), matched case-insensitively against the identity's sign-in name, display name and the names of its groups (an emergency-access group). An identity is break-glass when its provider says so (si_is_break_glass), when a pattern matches, when it carries the extension attribute and value you define (the list break_glass_attribute), or when it is excluded from ALL the enabled Conditional Access policies that would apply to it (being excluded from one policy is not enough). The CI build sets si_is_break_glass from it (engine/data-model/SI-BreakGlass.ps1), so the tier rules and the break-glass reports read it. Customer: lists.custom.json (a values list replaces this one; \"add\" extends it) -- Pro: the list's override (wizard).",
      "values": [
        "*break*glass*",
        "*emergency*access*",
        "*emergency*admin*",
        "*emergency*account*",
        "bg-*",
        "bg_*",
        "bg.*",
        "brk-*",
        "brk_*",
        "ea-admin*",
        "ea_admin*"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "break_glass_attribute",
      "type": "values",
      "description": "The EXTENSION ATTRIBUTE and VALUE that mark an emergency-access (break-glass) account in YOUR directory -- nothing is shipped; the list is empty until you define it. Each entry is attribute=value: the attribute is extensionAttribute1 to extensionAttribute15 (the user's on-premises extension attributes) or the name of a directory extension (extension_<application id without dashes>_<name>); the value is the text the attribute must hold (compared whole, upper and lower case alike; never empty). Example: extensionAttribute6=Break_Glass_Account. A user that carries one of the entries is break-glass (si_is_break_glass, with the entry in si_break_glass_attribute and the rule named in si_break_glass_reason). The attribute is read with the user only while an entry names it. Customer: lists.custom.json -- Pro: the list's override.",
      "values": []
    }
  ]
